{
  "section": "multidimensional-parallel-planning",
  "addedResources": [],
  "units": [
    {
      "id": "parallel-dimensions-factorization",
      "title": "The parallel dimensions and world-size factorization",
      "hours": 4,
      "target": [
        "explain",
        "derive"
      ],
      "topics": [
        "parallel-dimensions-dp-fsdp-tp-sp-cp-pp-and-ep",
        "world-size-factorization-and-divisibility-constraints"
      ],
      "outcomes": [
        "Identify the state or tensor dimension partitioned by each strategy and the communication it introduces.",
        "Derive world size from independent mesh axes without counting SP or a reused expert mesh twice.",
        "Distinguish data replicas, state-sharding groups and expert replicas in one composed plan."
      ],
      "why": "A planner must count physical ranks once even when their ownership changes between dense and expert layers. Start from the mechanisms you built in DT-3 through DT-7, not a list of parallelism labels.",
      "next": [
        {
          "unit": "distributed-training/multidimensional-parallel-planning/named-mesh-rank-coordinates",
          "note": "Turn independent axes into rank coordinates and groups."
        }
      ],
      "study": [
        {
          "resource": "ultrascale-5d-dt8",
          "title": "The strategies as one ownership map",
          "url": "https://nanotron-ultrascale-playbook.static.hf.space/index.html#5d_parallelism_in_a_nutshell",
          "locator": "5D Parallelism in a Nutshell, including Scope and focus and Summarizing it all"
        },
        {
          "resource": "torchfeather-mesh-dt8",
          "title": "Dense and sparse meshes reuse the same world",
          "url": "https://github.com/hkproj/torchfeather/blob/c416ab1ad0fd3567a0a6c8ad0da570c8fd6e55aa/torchfeather/distributed/parallel_dims.py",
          "locator": "`ParallelDims._validate` and `build_mesh`, especially the `dataloading`, `dense` and `sparse` mesh products",
          "depth": "Trace the products and ownership only. Do not copy the private mesh methods into your planner. The trainer pins PyTorch 2.11; this is an implementation convention, not a current public API contract."
        }
      ],
      "focus": [
        "For an independent dense mesh, write \(W = d_r d_s t c p\), with replicated DP, sharded DP, TP, CP and PP degrees respectively. FSDP describes state ownership on existing ranks, not another factor.",
        "Megatron-style SP uses the TP group for selected sequence-sharded activations. It does not add ranks. CP is a separate sequence axis in this mesh.",
        "TorchFeather uses \(d_s c t = d_e e t_e\) for expert-FSDP, EP and expert-TP degrees. The expert mesh refactors the same ranks; dense and expert state have different owners."
      ],
      "reproduce": "Closed-book, draw TorchFeather's three meshes for replicated DP 1, sharded DP 4, CP 2, TP 2, PP 1, EP 4 and expert TP 2. All use 16 ranks. Derive dense FSDP degree 8, expert FSDP degree 2 and independent data-replica degree 4; explain why multiplying by EP again would be wrong.",
      "traps": [
        "Treating every named strategy as an independent axis. Ask whether it introduces new ranks or changes ownership on existing ranks.",
        "Using the data-replica count for every gradient reduction. CP ranks contribute different tokens, and expert parameters have their own replication groups."
      ],
      "selfTest": [
        {
          "q": "Does enabling SP on an existing TP group double world size?",
          "a": "No. It changes selected activation layouts on those ranks."
        },
        {
          "q": "Why does CP not create new examples in the global batch?",
          "a": "Its ranks own pieces of the same sequences. Their token contributions must be combined, not counted as independent copies of those sequences."
        },
        {
          "q": "Can an expert parameter use the dense FSDP divisor without checking its mesh?",
          "a": "No. EP assigns different experts to ranks, so the ranks holding replicas of one expert can be a smaller group than the dense FSDP group."
        }
      ],
      "practice": [],
      "practiceNote": "The section mini-project covers this: trainlab's dense and expert ownership tables and world-size validation.",
      "stopWhen": "Your ownership table accounts for all 16 ranks once in each mesh and names the reduction group for dense and expert gradients.",
      "fit": {
        "verdict": "kept",
        "note": "The Playbook supplies the composition map; TorchFeather supplies exact mesh-product and expert-reuse constraints. Trainlab covers the ownership exercise."
      }
    },
